1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 {('PxB')} 3. Bc4 Qh4+ 4. Kf1 d5 5. Bxd5 g5 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. d4 c6 8. Bc4 Ne7 9. Nf3 Qh5 10. h4 h6 11. Kg1 Qg6 {('Q-Ka3')} 12. Ne5 Bxe5 13. dxe5 Qg7 14. hxg5 hxg5 15. Rxh8+ Qxh8 16. Qd6 Nd7 17. Bxf7+ Kxf7 18. e6+ Ke8 19. exd7+ Bxd7 20. Qc5 {('Q-B4')} Qh4 21. Bd2 Kf7 22. Be1 Qg4 23. Rd1 f3 24. Qf2 Qxg2+ {('PxP ch.') Source: Kent & Sussex Courier, 23 May 1902. Here the newspaper gives Black's move as '24...PxP ch.' followed by 'Resigns'. There is no legal pawn capture on the board so we're left wondering what happened. One possibility is that Black tried to play 24...fxg2 but, on being informed it was illegal, resigned the game. A second interpretation (which I favour) is that Black played Qxg2+ and, before White could reply, noticing that he was about to lose a piece, resigned.} (24... Qxg2+ 25. Qxg2 fxg2 26. Rxd7 {simply leaves Black a piece for a pawn down and with a hopeless position.}) 1-0

