1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. dxc5 Nc6 7. a3 Bxc5 8. Qg4 g6 9. Bd3 a6 10. Nf3 b5 11. h4 h5 12. Qg3 Nf8 13. b4 Bb6 14. Bb2 Bb7 15. O-O-O Rc8 16. Rhe1 Ne7 17. Ne4 $4 Nf5 $4 ({White's sacrifice is incomprehensible and totally unsound (as I realised at the instant I entered the move 17 Ne4, before later checking with an engine):} 17... dxe4 18. Bxb5+ axb5 19. Rxd8+ Rxd8 {and Black has more than enough material for the queen.}) 18. Qh3 $4 Nh7 $4 ({Nothing has changed. The sacrifice is still utterly unsound:} 18... dxe4 19. Bxb5+ axb5 20. Rxd8+ Rxd8 {and Black wins.}) 19. Nd6+ Nxd6 20. exd6 O-O 21. Bxg6 fxg6 22. Qxe6+ Rf7 23. Qxg6+ Kf8 24. Ng5 {Sources: Kent & Sussex Courier, 30 May 1902 (final move 24 Ne5); Western Morning News, 27 June 1902 (final move 24 Ng5). The newspapers don't comment on the moves played. The WMN gives a diagram of the position after 21 Bxg6 which tallies with the score as given here.} (24. Ne5 {is the final move given by the Kent & Sussex Courier, 30 May 1902.}) 1-0

