1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5 4. h4 g4 5. Ne5 d6 6. Nxg4 Be7 7. d4 Bxh4+ 8. Nf2 Qg5 9. Qf3 Nc6 10. Bb5 Bxf2+ 11. Kxf2 Qxb5 12. Nc3 Qg5 13. Bxf4 Qg4 {Source: Western Morning News, 5 June 1902.} (13... Qg4 {"The "Twins" that follow will be found an interesting pair of Kieseritsky gambits, which occurred at Tunbridge Wells between the American champion Pillsbury, and shall I say, the Totnes champion, without giving offence to the other members of the Totnes Chess Club, E. Douglas Fawcett. The first game was in simultaneous, and the second blindfold play by Pillsbury. In the first it will be seen - see diagram below - that Black has gained a piece, when Pillsbury pointed out that after} 14. Nd5 {and the exchange of queens the attack is by no means dead, and as he is the attacker he should know!" (Western Morning News) No further moves given, nor the result of the game.}) *

